{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1354233-non-setting-specific-supernatural-phenomena-creatures","id":1354233,"title":"Non-Setting Specific Supernatural Phenomena/Creatures?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-10T12:51:39-08:00","id":1354233,"title":"Non-Setting Specific Supernatural Phenomena/Creatures?","content":"Recently was going through the DtD book and realized that the Splinter timelines that are in Seattle just specific to Demons. That the Splinters can easily be transferred to other campaigns as well.<br /> <br /> It got me wondering what other Supernatural Phenomena/Creatures might be splat agnostic? These could be things that exist in books already or not.<br /> <br /> Recently I am trying to bring some r/nosleep stories into a game. But a lot of the entities don't fall under a specific type of splat so I've identify creatures like the Slenderman as &quot;Spooks&quot;. Entities that thrive from hiding in the shadows of the world. I'm relatively new to Chronicles of Darkness so I don't know what is or isn't already covered by the materials. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25291-donteatrawhagis","name":"DontEatRawHagis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T13:10:23-08:00","id":1354236,"title":"","content":"Claimed and Ridden are big ones often overlooked or considered 'werewolf only'. Near every splat can become one, or antagonized by them.<br /> <br /> Ghosts, angels, hosts, first edition initially established 'The Created' as the Imbued, bluebook golems and Frankensteins monsters. Idigam, too, are able to become anything and range towards large scale threats. Certainly easier to deal with as a werewolf, but not locked to them. There's also the Underworld and Shadow realms. Or you can use the Horror system to emulate any monsters you can dream of, like in Reighnhells <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/793173-the-horror-show\">Horror Show thread</a>, where many others contributed. We have a bunch of fanmade Horrors scattered around the forum in other threads, as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 12-10-2019, 06:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T14:45:15-08:00","id":1354244,"title":"","content":"The Chronicles of Darkness Rulebook contains a section on creating Horrors, which are miscellaneous forms of supernatural threats that are generally unrelated to preexisting character types or even each other. Sample Horrors include black-eyed kids, flesh-eating zombies, mirror people, a sinister wish-granting pond, and Mothman. That's probably the most direct comparison to your use of the word &quot;spook.&quot;<br /> <br /> Any type of character can encounter any other type of antagonist phenomenon if your ST has the relevant books; there's nothing preventing a mage from investigating a mystery centering around an angel of the God-Machine.<br /> <br /> Specific entities from one named gameline easy to introduce into other gamelines, due to being versatile in both form and theme, might include Deviant's Remade and Manticores (humans and animals, respectively, who have undergone any kind of transformation, ritual, mutation, or alteration which has unlocked supernatural power tied to debilitating conditions) and Hunter's slashers (humans who pursue murder so purely that their very identity becomes subsumed into their killing, who may or may not develop supernatural abilities). There are also supplements for the &quot;blue book&quot; or &quot;mortals&quot; line, Chronicles of Darkness supplements that aren't directly associated with specific gamelines, which tend to include various miscellaneous phenomena. That's where you'd find the demons of the Inferno, which are also probably easily incorporated into specific gameline encounters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T03:07:21-08:00","id":1354328,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Spirits and Ghosts are an obvious one (considering how many core books have their rules in.) You might not be advised to go too heavily on them unless your group has some tools that help with them, but there are tools out there for most game lines, (although finding tools to deal with spirits is a valid option for any line.)</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200bCryptids (strange animals with supernatural traits) have cropped up in at least Mage and Demon, Mummy has it's chimera's that basically fill the same role but fall slightly higher on the power scale.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200bHunters make pretty good antagonists for most game lines, particularly group like the Cheiron corporation or Ashwood Abbey.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200bBut basically it's one system, and if the ST has access to it (I once ran an astral realm plot for mortals.)</span></span></span></div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200b1) How many new concepts do you need to explain to your group for the plot to work?</span></span></span></div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">2) Does the motivation still work? - You can essentially divide a lot of antagonists to whether they are antagonists because they specifically hate X (Pandorans are a great example, being completely inert away from Prometheans), or because they are more generally reprehensible (such as Belial's Brood,) the later are reasonably transferable. <br /> \u200b3) Power level (Stumbling onto a Mummies tomb feels a nice non specific trigger, but a newly awakened Mummy is likely to be a huge problem for most groups.)<br /> \u200b4) How relevant are the tools your group have available for what they are dealing with? obvious ones include can they get into place, what methods do they have of finding information. Using the fractured time lines, a Mage with basic time is going to be able to get a handle on that far faster than any Vampire/</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200bThere are also a number of themes that crop up multiple times in the gamelines, leylines and places of power, 'magic' items relics/gadgets/fetishes etc (many of which while splat specific actually have very little limiting the appeal out of the splat.) If you are using either theme a group with the same interests from another line actually makes a good antagonist.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/711-mcgonigle","name":"mcgonigle","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T09:16:19-08:00","id":1354587,"title":"","content":"I'm making use of the 'splinter' concept in a different kind of way (in fact not being very familiar with Demon I'd only now call them that). Namely, I'm doing time-loops through alternate versions of the past/future. So far either conducted or planned:<br /> <br /> * A 2016-based apocalypse triggered by a character's time-shift (absence) from 1070.<br /> * The 1070-ish main timeline could be considered a very large shard as the characters were shifted from 2003 via an encounter with an object we can just call the &quot;Paradox Tome&quot; (Book of Thoth).<br /> * The &quot;new main&quot; 466+ timeline, triggered by the apocalypse above plus some hefty time-spirit wonkery and probably the G-M.<br /> * Arthurian future-techno 1066 thanks to a PC napping in Merlin's crystal cave.<br /> * A mysterious ritual that places individuals in a dream/travel to a past that strangely seems to accommodate them precisely, that non-travelers remember-ish after the participants awake, and that they can &quot;bring back things from&quot;. This may be creating splinters and then merging them, in effect. It's anchored on the blood of creatures who were there, then.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T10:45:08-08:00","id":1354610,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1354233-non-setting-specific-supernatural-phenomena-creatures?p=1354587#post1354587\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm making use of the 'splinter' concept in a different kind of way (in fact not being very familiar with Demon I'd only now call them that). Namely, I'm doing time-loops through alternate versions of the past/future. So far either conducted or planned:<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually a premise that the DtD Seattle supplement runs with. All time splinters are looping timelines, the examples they have are about 1 year loops I think. There is even an Apocalypse Vault that contains timelines where the apocalypse was successful that the G-M prevented.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25291-donteatrawhagis","name":"DontEatRawHagis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}